WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Practical Applications of the DFSS Methodology

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.500
Welcome back to this session on Design for Six Sigma.

3
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:11.500
Recall that we were looking about

4
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:16.133
how to take the X and Y measurements and form a simple scatterplot here.

5
00:00:16.133 --> 00:00:22.366
What we’re going to do, is we're looking how to perform tolerancing, or Design for Six Sigma.

6
00:00:22.366 --> 00:00:25.866
What we've done is inserted a line of best fit.

7
00:00:25.866 --> 00:00:30.166
Now, that's a line that you can see pretty much goes through the center of the data.

8
00:00:30.166 --> 00:00:34.166
So, if I draw an envelope around this data,

9
00:00:34.166 --> 00:00:38.166
we can kinda see that that line runs through that envelope.

10
00:00:38.166 --> 00:00:41.732
And what I've done. And we’re going to zero in here.

11
00:00:41.733 --> 00:00:48.166
I'm now going to turn this Y scale around on us here. As we zero in.

12
00:00:48.166 --> 00:00:53.799
We’re going to say for the sake of argument, that the customer

13
00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:58.233
set a lower specification limit on Y here.

14
00:00:58.233 --> 00:01:02.233
And an upper specification limit on Y there.

15
00:01:02.233 --> 00:01:05.766
And a target expectation here.

16
00:01:05.766 --> 00:01:09.732
So, that's the bandwidth that the customer wants.

17
00:01:09.733 --> 00:01:15.733
Now, what I do is come over,

18
00:01:15.733 --> 00:01:20.399
and I'm going to project this line. Now this isn’t precise.

19
00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:26.366
So, statisticians out there don't turn in your grave. I'm going to project, upper to upper

20
00:01:26.366 --> 00:01:32.832
and lower to lower here.

21
00:01:32.833 --> 00:01:38.833
See, as I project over. I’m going to turn it around.

22
00:01:38.833 --> 00:01:44.966
We’re going to zoom back out a little bit here and I'm going to project down.

23
00:01:44.966 --> 00:01:51.166
Just like this. As so.

24
00:01:51.166 --> 00:01:54.199
So, notice the correlation, the lower

25
00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:57.933
element of X goes to the lower element of Y. Upper to upper.

26
00:01:57.933 --> 00:02:03.466
Now, what is this saying? Here comes what's so, so vital.

27
00:02:03.466 --> 00:02:07.899
I would now in engineering this, say this.

28
00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:13.400
That there is the upper specification limit on X.

29
00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:16.766
There is the lower specification limit.

30
00:02:16.766 --> 00:02:23.566
And I project over and down, and there's the target value of X.

31
00:02:23.566 --> 00:02:27.699
So, what that’s saying is this. Now, listen to this. Here’s what’s cool.

32
00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:31.700
If this is the capability through here,

33
00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:38.733
that transfers up and over to the capability here.

34
00:02:38.733 --> 00:02:45.266
See, what I'm doing is reversing it. I'm starting here and projecting down

35
00:02:45.266 --> 00:02:49.499
to the temperature. Remember, X was temperature.

36
00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:54.933
So, this tells me the range in which I must operate,

37
00:02:54.933 --> 00:03:01.666
process, say, process operation 1, process operation 2, process operation 3.

38
00:03:01.666 --> 00:03:07.066
And out comes, here is the product with the circuit board.

39
00:03:07.066 --> 00:03:13.699
If I want that to operate properly here, that solder joint’s gotta be strong enough

40
00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:19.333
to hold up to do that. And this says if that's the pull strength expectation.

41
00:03:19.333 --> 00:03:25.099
Then that's the temperature range I have to hold.

42
00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:28.466
To make that happen.

43
00:03:28.466 --> 00:03:32.366
Well what I'm doing is Designing for Six Sigma.

44
00:03:32.366 --> 00:03:35.366
Because I know this.

45
00:03:35.366 --> 00:03:42.066
If I find a temperature control method or device

46
00:03:42.066 --> 00:03:45.566
that stops it from varying over time,

47
00:03:45.566 --> 00:03:49.899
this little distribution here, is going to get more narrow.

48
00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:52.633
To such an extent,

49
00:03:52.633 --> 00:03:59.299
that I want that to be Six Sigma capable, ± 6σ.

50
00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:03.300
6σ to the right, 6σ to the left.

51
00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:08.566
As that happens guess what? Those 6σ
limits transfers up and over.

52
00:04:08.566 --> 00:04:12.566
That means I get Six Sigma performance out here.

53
00:04:12.566 --> 00:04:17.499
Now, I just reverse the process if I'm Designing for Six Sigma.

54
00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:21.433
I define Six Sigma here and project back and it tells me

55
00:04:21.433 --> 00:04:25.433
where I have to run my process to achieve my goals.

56
00:04:25.433 --> 00:04:30.933
So in essence, as I set my design standards that way, I improve the yields

57
00:04:30.933 --> 00:04:34.933
at each step, which improves the rolled throughput.

58
00:04:34.933 --> 00:04:38.166
And that's what we call Designing for Six Sigma.

59
00:04:38.166 --> 00:04:43.166
Thank you for choosing SSMI.

